<p class="lead">Many contractors run more than one company: a general contracting business, a specialty trade shop, a residential arm. Each one usually ends up with its own tools and its own way of working. Multi-company construction software puts them on one platform while keeping each company's books apart. Here is how it should be set up.</p>
<h2 id="the-cost-of-separate-systems">The cost of separate systems</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The same subcontractor is entered three times, with three copies of their insurance.</li>
  <li>Office staff log in and out of different tools all day.</li>
  <li>Estimates, contracts and invoices are retyped into <a href="/integrations/quickbooks-online">QuickBooks</a>.</li>
  <li>Nobody sees all the companies' jobs and money in one view.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-to-share-and-what-to-keep-separate">What to share and what to keep separate</h2>
